WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:05.280
Trying to stay healthy can seem&nbsp;
like a full-time job sometimes.&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.960
Especially with all the conflicting&nbsp;
information that’s out there.&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.880
But, I’m here to make that job a little easier.&nbsp;

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:18.043
Welcome to the Nutrition Facts podcast,
I’m your host Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:23.040
I knew next to nothing about cold plunges&nbsp;
when I started researching them and&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.080
honestly, they turned out to be
much more interesting than I thought.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.680
In our first story we look at the effects&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.080
of cold water immersion on the
recovery of athletic performance.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:33.360
In the last decade,

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.600
cold-water immersion
has emerged

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:36.800
as one of the most popular

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.520
post-exercise
recovery strategies

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.760
utilized among athletes
during training and competition.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.480
This primarily stems
from the belief

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:47.920
that cold-water immersion

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.680
facilitates aspects
of recovery and regeneration,

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.440
thereby conferring
a potential training

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.280
and performance advantage.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.640
But is it a friend, foe,
or just futile?

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:05.680
The cold truth...
is that local cryotherapy,

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:06.960
meaning like an ice pack,

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.960
does not appear
to diminish soreness

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.360
or accelerate
the recovery strength.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.920
Local cryotherapy
does not seem to contribute

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.960
to the improvement
of delayed onset muscle soreness

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:18.240
or muscle weakness

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.200
associated with
exercise-induced muscle damage,

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.720
but what about cold plunges?

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.880
If you look at
the largest systematic review

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.120
and meta-analysis
of the effects of cold plunges

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.600
on the recovery
of physical performance,

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.720
compiling the results
of more than five dozen

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:37.280
randomized, controlled,
or crossover studies,

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.360
after cold-water immersion,

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:42.080
people felt less sore
after a workout,

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:44.640
at one hour later,
six hours later,

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.600
one day, two days,
and three days later,

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:49.680
but not after four days.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.080
They also felt less fatigued,

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:55.840
particularly in the hour
or hours immediately afterwards.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:57.440
And, after three days,

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.480
they reported feeling
more recovered

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.760
than those who hadn’t done
the cold plunges.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.160
But were
they actually more recovered

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.320
using objective measures?

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:11.760
Endurance was significantly
improved an hour later,

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.280
but not after that.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.120
Jump performance was impaired
six hours after the cold plunge

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.975
but was significantly improved
24 hours later.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.920
If we put
all the studies together,

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:28.080
there may be a small benefit
favoring cold plunges,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:29.600
but only about one in five

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.840
found statistically
significant benefit,

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:32.960
one found harm,

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.160
and the vast majority
found no significant effect.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:37.760
Still, enough of the studies

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.200
leaned in the direction
of benefit to tip the scale.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.280
In terms of sprint performance,

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.160
there was an initial
deterioration in performance,

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:47.520
but then none after that,

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.000
and a significant increase
in strength recovery

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.600
the next day.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.120
This coincides with a lessening
of muscular damage indicators

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.200
the next day, lasting through
days two and three.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.760
What are the best parameters
of cold plunges

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.720
in terms of timing,
temperature, and duration?

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:07.120
There really isn’t
a "gold standard,"

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.880
but the first systematic review

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.040
concluded that
cold-water immersion

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.760
at a temperature
between 11 and 15°C

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.280
for 11 15 minutes was best.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.680
That’s like
in the 50s Fahrenheit.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.520
However,
since that first review,

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:22.880
there’s been
a bunch more studies,

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.640
and the updated review

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:28.000
found that the water temperature
didn’t seem to matter,

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:29.520
whether severely cold at

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:34.000
5 to 9 degrees Celsius
(in the Fahrenheit 40s)

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.360
or that moderately cold
at 10 to 15°C,

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.680
but the immersion duration
did seem to matter.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:44.160
Immersions lasting
less than ten minutes

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:45.760
presented better results

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:48.080
and plunging
for more than 16 minutes

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:49.760
didn’t seem to help at all.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:52.640
And it’s better to immerse
immediately following exercise

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:54.640
rather than waiting.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:56.400
Now, in most of these studies,

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:58.880
researchers used
passive recovery

00:03:58.880 --> 00:03:59.840
in the control groups,

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.000
meaning people were randomized
to cold plunges

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.840
or to just, like, sit
in a chair for 20 minutes.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:09.840
But who exercises intensely
and then just sits in a chair?

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.160
Many athletes use
active recovery

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:15.440
in the form
of a low-intensity "warm down"

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:17.280
to recover after exercise,

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:20.240
like walking around a bit
to keep the blood flowing,

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:22.960
getting rid of some
of that lactic acid buildup.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:23.920
So, instead,

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.640
what about randomizing people
to cold-water immersion

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.600
versus normal active recovery?

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.080
That might be
a fairer comparison.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:36.560
And in that case,
the cold plunge didn’t work.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.120
It was no more effective
than active recovery.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.560
Okay, but that’s just one study.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:45.600
Let’s look at a meta-analysis
of all such studies

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:47.520
comparing the effects
of cold-water immersion

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.160
with other recovery modalities.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:51.840
In terms of muscle soreness,

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:55.040
cold-water immersion
didn’t seem to work any better

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:59.040
than active recovery or any
of the other recovery methods,

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.280
including warm-water immersion.

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:03.920
Same for the recovery
of muscular power.

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:05.520
No significant difference

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.920
between cold plunging
and active recovery,

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:09.280
contrast water therapy,

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:11.600
which is like alternating
hot and cold,

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:12.880
warm-water immersion,

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:15.680
air cryotherapy,
like in a cryochamber,

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:19.280
massage,
or electrical stimulation.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:21.120
If it’s all the same,

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:24.720
I think I might prefer
the massage to the plunge.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.600
Same for the recovery of muscle
strength, no difference.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:31.440
Immersing in cold water
did seem to beat out warm water

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.520
on perceived recovery, though,
but nothing else,

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.160
and it was the same with
flexibility, no difference,

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.160
and the same with measures
of muscle damage,

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:41.680
though electrifying your legs

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:44.800
did seem to work better
than the cold water.

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:47.600
The bottom line
is that all the recovery methods

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:50.000
seem to achieve about the same.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.920
So, a cold plunge
may be better

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:54.720
than just sitting down
after intense exercise,

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:56.800
but there’s a wide range
of other practices

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.520
that appear to be
just as effective.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:02.400
But any time doing something
beats out doing nothing,

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:07.360
you always have to ask if it
might just be a placebo effect.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:10.080
You don’t know
if the perceived benefits

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:12.320
of cold plunges
are all in your head

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:15.360
until you put it to the test

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:18.560
with a placebo-controlled trial,

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:21.040
but how do you even do that?

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:24.400
We’ll find out, next.

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:28.560
Cold-water immersion
is a post-exercise modality

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:30.640
purported to enhance
physical recovery

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:32.560
following strenuous exercise,

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:33.920
and people certainly report

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:35.440
feeling less sore,
less fatigued,

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:37.840
and more recovered
after cold plunging,

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:40.000
but the vast majority
of cryotherapy studies

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:43.040
have been conducted using
a do-nothing control group,

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:44.560
like just sitting in a chair,

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:47.280
and have not taken
expectancy effect

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:49.440
or treatment belief
into account,

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.280
meaning maybe the cold plunge
only works

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:53.200
because people think it works.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:56.640
In other words, could it
just be the placebo effect?

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:59.280
Practitioners didn’t
just feel better, though;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:02.880
in some cases,
they performed better too,

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:06.320
but that also could be
a placebo effect.

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:08.800
Give athletes placebos,

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:13.600
and there aren’t only effects
on perceived exertion,

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:16.880
for example,
but on actual muscle power,

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:19.120
running speed,
and even heart rate.

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:20.720
Placebos, like sugar pills,

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:22.720
that are deceptively claimed

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:25.520
to be some
performance-enhancing drug,

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:28.480
actually enhance performance.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:31.280
Or, give one set of people
a capsule of corn starch

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:33.280
and say it’s going
to make them run faster,

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:35.280
and another the same capsule

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:38.000
and tell them it’s going
to make them run slower,

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:39.680
and, you guessed it,

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:42.080
the it-will-make-you-faster
group ran faster,

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:45.440
and the it-will-make-you-slower
group ran slower.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:46.960
Give people a sugar pill
and tell them

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.280
it’s a strong combination
of amino acids

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:51.200
with immediate effects
on strength,

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:55.200
and they magically leg
and bench press more,

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.400
until they’re informed
they were lied to

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:00.240
and learn
it was just a sugar pill,

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:01.360
and just like that,

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:05.200
their performance enhancement
disappeared.

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:07.600
You can even get
a dose-response effect.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:08.880
When people were told
they were getting

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:12.240
about 300 mg of caffeine,
600 mg of caffeine,

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:13.520
or a placebo,

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:16.720
even though
everybody got placebos,

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:19.760
the ones who were told
they got the placebo did worse,

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:20.880
and there was increased power

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:22.720
in the lower
fake-caffeine group,

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:25.920
and even more in the higher
fake-caffeine group,

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:28.080
with all the fake-caffeinated
participants

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:31.040
even reporting
caffeine-related side effects.

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:33.120
Once people are told
they are taking a substance

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:36.480
that will enhance performance,
all sorts of things kick in:

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:39.520
belief, pain sensation,
expectancy, and arousal,

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:42.720
all of which
may impact performance.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:47.120
No wonder there’s a gazillion
dollar sports supplement market

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:51.040
selling tons of drinks,
powders, and supplements.

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:55.280
They may work, but maybe not
any better than a sugar pill,

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:58.240
which would certainly be
a lot cheaper.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:08:59.680
For cold-water immersion,

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:02.560
it’s difficult to administer
a true placebo,

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:04.480
but researchers cited a study

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:07.360
that did include
a placebo treatment,

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:08.800
and the effect
of cold-water immersion

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:11.920
was found to be no bigger
than the placebo effect.

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:14.880
There have actually been
five such studies to date.

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:16.720
Here’s the one
they were referring to.

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:18.080
Thirty guys performed

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:21.040
an acute high-intensity
interval training session,

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:22.960
comprised of four
30-second sprints,

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:25.040
immediately followed
by one of the following

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:28.320
three 15-minute
recovery conditions:

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:29.360
cold-water immersion

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:31.360
at 10.3°C +/- 0.2°C,
about 50°F,

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:36.640
a warm, near-body temperature
water immersion placebo,

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:41.040
or a warm, near-body temperature
water-immersion control group.

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:42.800
Uh, hold on.

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:45.120
What’s the difference
between the placebo group

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:47.040
and the control group?

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:48.560
In the placebo group,

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:51.360
the researchers squirted
something in the water

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:54.240
(actually just some soap)
and led the participants

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:58.720
to believe it had special
recovery enhancement properties.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:01.840
This study demonstrated
that influencing one’s belief

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:03.600
in the efficacy
of warm-water immersion

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:06.080
as an ergogenic aid
in recovery

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:09.760
can account for an approximate
13 percent improvement

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:13.840
in muscle strength 48 hours
after high-intensity exercise;

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:16.960
so, it’s important for coaches
and sports scientists alike

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:19.360
to educate their athletes
on the benefits of recovery

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:23.120
and to also encourage belief
in the practice.

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:27.360
Wait, are they encouraging them
to lie to athletes?

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:30.560
Hey, if that lie is going
to increase performance

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:32.480
by 13 percent.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:33.520
Here are the other four

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:36.160
placebo-controlled
cold-plunge studies.

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:38.240
Cold-water immersion offers

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:40.960
no functional or even
perceptual benefit

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:42.960
compared to a sham intervention

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:45.000
during an eight-week
resistance training program.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:47.360
Here, researchers told people
in the control group

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:50.240
that their protein supplements
were tweaked

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:52.720
and that was enough
to completely eliminate

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:54.240
any significant effect,

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:56.160
and had the training program
gone longer,

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:57.520
they would expect
the cold-immersion group

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:01.040
to actually shrink
long-term gains in muscle mass,

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:03.440
or strength, or both.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:06.560
The next was cold-water
immersion after a soccer match,

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:09.280
randomized to cold plunge,
passive rest recovery,

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:11.840
or a quote-unquote
"recovery" beverage,

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:13.520
which was just a placebo.

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:16.480
And both the cold plunge
and placebo were more effective

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:17.680
than the rest condition.

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:21.680
Doing something made people feel
better than doing nothing,

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:25.440
but the cold plunge did no
better than the fake beverage,

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:26.800
demonstrating that indeed

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:29.760
cold-water immersion reduces
muscle soreness,

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:32.400
but this beneficial effect
was a manifestation

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:35.840
of the placebo effect.

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:37.680
What about whole-body
cryotherapy--

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:40.560
like sitting in a cryochamber
at 100 below zero F (-73°C)--

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:45.280
versus cold plunge or placebo
after resistance exercise?

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:47.600
Neither cryotherapy intervention
was more effective

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:50.800
than the placebo treatment
at accelerating recovery,

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:54.160
neither was more effective
as a pill of cornstarch,

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:56.480
as long as you tell people
that cornstarch pill

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:59.760
is a branched-chain
amino acid supplement.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:02.720
Oh my God, are athletes
that gullible?

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:07.680
No wonder these supplement
companies make off like bandits.

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:09.920
The majority of group
comparisons revealed

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:13.040
unclear, trivial, or unfavorable
effects of cryotherapy

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:14.880
compared to the placebo
intervention,

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:15.600
meaning in some ways,

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:18.720
the magical cornstarch pill
worked better,

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:20.640
contradicting much
of the previous literature,

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:23.040
but that’s because
much of the previous literature

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:25.440
compared cold-water immersion
to nothing,

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:27.840
just like sitting in a chair.

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:30.800
And finally,
recovery following a marathon:

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:32.640
a comparison
of cold-water immersion,

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:34.000
whole-body cryotherapy,

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:35.360
and a placebo control group

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:38.480
given a fake tart cherry
juice beverage.

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:40.720
Neither cold treatment,
whether wet or dry,

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:43.040
was more effective
than a placebo

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:44.800
at improving
functional recovery

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:48.320
or even perceptions of training
stress following a marathon.

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:50.960
In fact, the placebo worked
even better.

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:53.200
Cold water worked better
than the cryotherapy,

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:58.000
but they both did worse than
the fake fruit-flavored drink.

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:00.560
These findings lend
further evidence to suggest

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:03.600
that treatment belief
and the placebo effect

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:04.960
may be largely responsible

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:08.880
for the beneficial effects
of cryotherapy on recovery.

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:12.480
But what about the objective
drop in muscle damage indicators

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:14.160
after cold-water immersion?

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:16.000
It’s been reasoned
that reducing

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:17.360
muscle temperature
and blood flow

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:19.600
may dampen the local
inflammatory response

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:22.320
and thereby boost
post-exercise recovery.

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:26.560
What is striking is the
assumption that inflammation

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:28.960
needs to be dampened
or suppressed.

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:31.040
Isn’t inflammation
a crucial component

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:34.800
of the adaptive response
of skeletal muscle to training?

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:38.560
And cold plunges aren’t
actually anti-inflammatory.

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:41.120
Where did we even get
that idea from?

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:42.880
It was from studies
on lab animals

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:46.080
with induced
gross muscle injuries,

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:50.560
which isn’t exactly the same
as just exercising strenuously.

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:52.960
Okay, but there’s still
that reduced muscle damage,

00:13:52.960 --> 00:13:54.960
even if doesn’t affect
inflammation

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:57.280
or muscle function recovery.

00:13:57.280 --> 00:14:00.480
But doesn’t exercise-induced
muscle damage play a role

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:02.640
in skeletal muscle growth?

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:05.360
Might dampening the damage
be throwing cold water

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:07.440
on muscle growth?

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:12.080
We’ll find out next.

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:13.040
In my last video,

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:15.280
I talked about how
the benefits of cold plunges

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:17.920
appear to be due
to the placebo effect.

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:20.560
But cold-water immersion
isn’t just futile

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:24.640
but may be a foe when it comes
to our muscle mass.

00:14:24.640 --> 00:14:27.680
A key take-home message is
that if the goal of training

00:14:27.680 --> 00:14:29.760
is to build muscle mass
and strength,

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:34.160
regular cold plunges after
exercise may not be beneficial.

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:36.640
It all started
with this study in 2006,

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:38.880
showing that cold-water
immersion of muscles

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:41.840
may actually interfere
with regenerative processes

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:45.520
and retard rather than support
the desired improvements

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:47.520
of muscular performance.

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:50.000
Since then, independent
corroborating evidence

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:53.440
has continued to accumulate
that post-exercise cooling

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:56.480
diminishes muscle adaptations
to strength training,

00:14:56.480 --> 00:15:00.160
leading to punny
click-bait titles like

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:03.680
"Cryotherapy: Are we freezing
the benefits of exercise?",

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:06.240
"Is the ice bath
finally melting?",

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:10.880
"The cold truth," "Cooling down
the use of cryotherapy."

00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:13.600
Here’s a typical study showing
that cold plunges

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:16.000
substantially attenuate
long-term gains

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:18.320
in muscle mass and strength.

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:19.520
Twelve weeks
of strength training

00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:21.200
with just typical
active recovery

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:24.640
led to a significant increase
in muscle mass in the quads,

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:26.880
but the same amount of training
with cold plunges

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:28.640
barely moved the needle.

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:32.080
And this manifested in
significantly less strength,

00:15:32.080 --> 00:15:34.800
whether we’re talking
maximum leg-press strength,

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:36.560
knee extension,
isometric torque,

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:39.040
or rate of force development.

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:41.840
Note that smaller muscles
don’t necessarily lead

00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:42.880
to less strength.

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:45.360
In this study,
the blunted muscle fiber growth

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:48.640
didn’t translate to
impaired strength development,

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:51.360
but post-exercise cold plunges
should be avoided

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:54.240
if you do actually want
bigger muscles.

00:15:54.240 --> 00:15:56.320
But there are folks,
like long-distance runners,

00:15:56.320 --> 00:15:58.320
who may not want
that extra bulk;

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:01.840
so, cold plunges may be a way
to keep their muscles smaller.

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:04.160
This is probably
the most rigorous study

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:05.680
involving measuring muscle mass

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:08.640
using the gold standard,
MRI imaging,

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:11.600
and actually taking muscle
biopsies before and after.

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:14.000
And, while both groups gained
muscle mass and strength

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:14.880
following training,

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:17.440
these gains were
significantly smaller

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:19.280
in the cold-water
immersion group,

00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:21.200
compared with
the active recovery group.

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:24.160
And the cross-sectional area
of fast-twitch muscle fibers

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:27.120
also increased
only in the control group.

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:27.920
There have been all sorts

00:16:27.920 --> 00:16:29.760
of published points
and counterpoints

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:33.760
about the cold-water dampening
effect on hypertrophy gains.

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:36.160
Here’s a systematic review
with meta-analysis

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:38.000
of all such studies.

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:42.240
A harmful effect of cold-water
immersion was verified

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:45.200
for every single
strength parameter measure:

00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:48.800
one-rep maximum,
maximal isometric strength,

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:50.960
strength endurance,
and ballistic efforts

00:16:50.960 --> 00:16:54.000
all reduced by cold plunges.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:56.880
So, it appears useless
for endurance training

00:16:56.880 --> 00:16:59.680
and deleterious
for strength training.

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:01.280
Why are people still doing it?

00:17:01.280 --> 00:17:05.680
Here’s a 2024 meta-analysis,
and the title says it all:

00:17:05.680 --> 00:17:09.120
Throwing cold water
on muscle growth.

00:17:09.120 --> 00:17:11.360
This may be because
cold-water immersion

00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:15.200
puts the chill
on muscle protein synthesis,

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:17.440
both in the hours immediately
following exercise

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:19.840
and during weeks
of strength training.

00:17:19.840 --> 00:17:21.120
Another explanation

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:22.960
for cold-induced
reductions in strength

00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:25.600
is that cold-water immersion
constricts the arteries

00:17:25.600 --> 00:17:27.840
and causes a reduction
in blood flow

00:17:27.840 --> 00:17:30.640
which is important for muscle
strength and development.

00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:33.600
A 50 percent drop
in femoral blood flow,

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:36.080
compared to warmer water
or control.

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:39.280
Reductions in muscle blood flow
reduces the oxygen supply,

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:42.480
which plays a role in
the decreased muscle mass, too.

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:47.040
Cold plunges also blunt
the testosterone response.

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:50.240
So, the impaired testosterone
response may be another reason

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:53.280
for the attenuated strength
and muscle growth.

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:56.640
So, cold plunges following
resistance exercise sessions

00:17:56.640 --> 00:17:59.040
should be discouraged,

00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:02.400
but they are said not to impair
aerobic training.

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:03.920
That’s not always the case,
though,

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:08.000
as some studies show a reduction
in aerobic fitness as well.

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:11.920
This study found that muscle
warming was better than cooling,

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:14.720
thought due to the slowing
of glycogen resynthesis

00:18:14.720 --> 00:18:16.160
in the cold.

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:19.440
Mean power output was
better preserved hours after

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:21.920
an all-out arm-cycling exercise.

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:24.480
The cold did worse than control,

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:25.840
and after the muscle heating,

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:29.920
there was hardly any decrease
in power output at all.

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:33.360
So, wait, should we instead
be taking a warm bath or sauna

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:34.080
after exercise

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:36.160
instead of an ice-water bath?

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:38.400
Regardless,
cryotherapy is apparently

00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:42.480
not as cool as it seems.

